{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-8683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/assets/caa6662d8349751e35bcb1af4196ad043b66ea64f21805eb6ca9b14435d38458.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-8683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad70ee37af7a94733925a9ee2b306d78af09939ad6f742960c6e3b68a526b3ee"} {"question" "q-8683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-8683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{25}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-8683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-8690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{6}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ rounded to the nearest integer?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-8690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{6})=1.738\\,244…≈1.738\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.738$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.738.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -1.738 & ≈1.404.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.404.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/assets/392ae25b9a2aa47e5292d7e623922803891b52099d20525f888daca92642ce88.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.404 \\\\  & =4.546\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =4.546-2\\pi  \\\\  & ≈-1.738\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈1.738\\cdot (-1.738)≈-3\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-8690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad70ee37af7a94733925a9ee2b306d78af09939ad6f742960c6e3b68a526b3ee"} {"question" "q-8690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-8690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "-9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-8690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-72838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{4}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-72838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{4}{7})=0.962\\,550…≈0.962\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.962\\,6$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.962\\,6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.962\\,6.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.962\\,6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/assets/b2cd7e76526632d8507ba484246a560829882e7f10d9814f01593f7a48d9a2ac.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.962\\,6 \\\\  & =5.320\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈0.962\\,6\\cdot 5.320\\,6≈5.1\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-72838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad70ee37af7a94733925a9ee2b306d78af09939ad6f742960c6e3b68a526b3ee"} {"question" "q-72838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-72838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4.8"] ["math" "4.9"] ["math" "5.1"] ["math" "5.5"] ["math" "6.1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-72838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5.1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-72842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-72842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the second solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=-1,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={90}^{∘}-{180}^{∘}=-{90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-72842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad70ee37af7a94733925a9ee2b306d78af09939ad6f742960c6e3b68a526b3ee"} {"question" "q-72842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-72842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-{45}^{∘},\\,{45}^{∘}"] ["math" "x=-{60}^{∘},\\,{60}^{∘}"] ["math" "x=-{60}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}"] ["math" "x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{45}^{∘}"] ["math" "x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-72842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-338740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The solution to the equation $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3=0$ for ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘}$ is $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-338740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)={0}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${0}^{∘}$ does not lie inside the required domain, we must find other solutions.\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find other solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx=n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find a solution in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1\\cdot {360}^{∘}={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={360}^{∘}$ is the only solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-338740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad70ee37af7a94733925a9ee2b306d78af09939ad6f742960c6e3b68a526b3ee"} {"question" "q-338740" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"} {"question" "q-338740" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "360"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/state.json" "file_sha256" "7153ab8a17e9f2fe81c6b04f0166a79f849f58b7f32919d66d35f1b464c6fe2c"}] "usage" {} "basis" 658 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
